const unit4Deck = [

  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "How do IT project teams differ from most organizational teams, and why does this matter? (mnemonic: Tired Dogs Can't Go)", "answer": "Teams are Temporary: they exist only for the duration of the project and often have transient and/or part-time members. Diverse: as diverse as the multidisciplinary nature of the project. Cross-cultural: e.g. in partnerships where more than one organization is involved. Geographically dispersed (especially in large projects): they may communicate through virtual means with all the implied limitations (time constraints, disengagement, etc.) (Niazi et al., 2016). With such diverse constitutions, project teams need strong ties and motivation to stay engaged and committed to project work."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "Define motivation and name the three factors typically required to motivate team members. (mnemonic: AAP)", "answer": "Motivation is expressed as the will to perform, and drives the team's effort and persistence in a certain direction. It is driven by team members' values of what is important in the context of personal aspirations for the environment they operate in (Patanakul, Pinto & Pinto, 2016). Three factors: Achievement: the human desire to be recognized as an achiever; Affiliation: the need to feel wanted and connected in the environment that the project addresses; Power: control of people or activities within one's sphere of influence."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "Why is a project vision a strong motivator for project teams?", "answer": "Considering the special characteristics of project teams, one strong motivator is a project vision that can bond the team in pursuing a shared future (Shenhar, 2015). It helps to clarify the project's purpose and eliminate confusion about the direction and goals of the team. It is an essential starting point for inspired action and dedication that (in addition to the satisfaction of being part of the team) provides focus and a sense of belonging to something significant and meaningful."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "Distinguish between a vision and a vision statement. What form do vision statements take? Give the cRobot examples.", "answer": "Vision (learn verbatim): an all-encompassing idea that uplifts individuals; a belief-forming proposition that carries a lot of emotional weight. Vision statement: a concise declaration of the picture that can act as a mantra for communication purposes between team members; it formalizes the big picture and helps the team see and understand the direction they will take. Sidebar: an indication of the future of the company which indicates a direction for its growth. Form: action-deliverable-criteria. cRobot: action 'design', deliverable 'robot', criteria 'carry a (warm) cup of coffee': 'Design a robot that follows its owner while carrying their warm cup of coffee.' This expresses direction pragmatically; strong vision statements are more impactful in an 'inspirational' sense: 'Design a smart assistant that cares enough about its owner to follow them around while carrying a warm cup of coffee.' It sounds more like a marketing message but projects an image that appeals to team members or people intending to join. 'Smart' suggests a high-tech product with AI and smart features; 'assistant' raises the level of service. These words might drive the team to consider casual language in the robot's speech software or extra human-like facial features; the remainder helps to visualize or dream of something interesting."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "What is a mission statement, how does it differ from the vision, and what is a project strategy?", "answer": "Apart from its inspirational character, the vision's function is to guide the mission and strategy the team will follow. The mission statement expresses the vision in more practical terms; the first (pragmatic) cRobot statement is more like a mission than a vision. Missions need to be concrete, including goal-oriented language and measurable objectives. Missions are embodiments of the vision: they guide the daily activities of everyone in the project/organization and form the benchmark for evaluating whether activities help achieve the mission. While the vision is more like an external mantra for all stakeholders, the mission is an internal one for the project team. Having a clear vision and mission is critical in developing the strategic elements of the project. A project strategy normally includes the short and long-term goals of the project and how these goals will be achieved (Tonchia, 2018); it is focused on present actions and outcomes needed to move toward the project product or service. Communication of vision, mission and strategy starts from the top (the PM) and spreads down to team members and other stakeholders; here PMs need to take on a leadership role and inspire everyone to align with them."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "MCQ: Which statement about vision and mission is correct according to the coursebook?\nA) The mission is an external mantra for all stakeholders, the vision an internal one for the team.\nB) The vision is an external mantra for all stakeholders, the mission an internal one for the team.\nC) Both are internal to the project team.\nD) The mission carries emotional weight; the vision contains measurable objectives.", "answer": "B. The vision is an external mantra for all stakeholders; the mission is internal for the project team. The vision carries emotional weight; the mission uses goal-oriented language and measurable objectives."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "How does PMBOK define leadership? Compare transformational and transactional leadership and state what research concludes.", "answer": "PMBOK (Larson & Gray, 2015), learn verbatim: leadership is developing a vision and a strategy, and then motivating people toward achieving that vision and strategy. PM literature tends to take a systematized and transactional view of leadership; dominant models emphasize routine, order and structure. With rare exceptions like Keegan and Hartog (2004), which examine transformational leadership and employee motivation and commitment in project teams, most literature focuses on transactional forms. Transformational leadership focuses on how leaders inspire followers by articulating a strong vision and creating a culture of trust and empowerment (sidebar: used to motivate employees and create positive change within the company). Transactional theories consider leadership as a transaction between leader and followers; in pure form it is a rewards-based approach, closer to management than leadership; it sets expectations and goals for task completion associated with rewards and recognition; it is a form of dependency-building to ensure task success is directly related to the success of the individuals involved (sidebar: implements a structured environment where employees can work with direction). Most research concludes that new models of leadership need to be developed that focus on the temporary organizations of project teams to reflect the short-term, transient nature of such work. (tutor-only) Transactional = task-oriented orders ('you do task A, you task B'); transformational = consultative, asks for ideas and opinions, empowers. She also mentioned contingency theory (no single style fits all situations; adapt style to the situation), which is not in the coursebook."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "What are the benefits and challenges of blending transformational and transactional leadership? (benefits mnemonic: Flexible Leaders Communicate Expectations)", "answer": "Benefits (Harkiolakis, 2016): easier to adapt to different situations; more flexible in the execution of command, as a blended approach allows personal interactions and the exercise of authority as necessary; effective for communicating information and vision at the organizational level; can convey high expectations while appealing to the core values and creative nature of employees. Combinations aim to take advantage of the strengths of one approach to eliminate the weaknesses of another. Challenges: if the leader is not experienced enough, one style might be applied to situations suited to the other; it might be perceived as an attempt at manipulation; leaders might be viewed as unstable if they switch abruptly between styles."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "How does the coursebook distinguish leaders from managers in projects?", "answer": "At times both roles communicate similar information, but the prime responsibility of leaders is the effective communication of the vision, while that of managers is the communication of the objectives for accomplishing that vision. To achieve these goals both roles can motivate, inspire, discipline, plan, supervise, mentor, etc. The challenge in project management is that there is extensive overlap between the two, and one might dominate the other depending on the project life cycle phase and the challenges facing the team. Blended leadership styles could allow more interplay between the roles of leader and manager, potentially addressing shortcomings of existing theories."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "Describe Figure 26 (Combining Transactional and Transformational Styles) and the recommended practice.", "answer": "One popular combination applies transformational and transactional leadership for alternating between empowerment and control. Praise and recognition behaviors reflect a blend of both: they are a personal interaction (transformational) because they involve individual attention, and an impersonal interaction (transactional) because they can be based solely on employee performance. Figure 26/slide: transformational = individual attention, used to motivate employees and create positive change; transactional = impersonal interaction, structured environment where employees work with direction. Recommended practice: use transactional behaviors as a foundation and display transformational behaviors for motivation and support."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "MCQ: What does the coursebook recommend when combining leadership styles?\nA) Use transformational behaviors as a foundation and transactional behaviors for control\nB) Use transactional behaviors as a foundation and display transformational behaviors for motivation and support\nC) Switch abruptly between styles to keep the team alert\nD) Avoid blending because it is perceived as manipulation", "answer": "B. Abrupt switching is a listed challenge (leaders look unstable); perceived manipulation is a risk, not a reason to avoid blending."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "Why is project culture important, and can a PM create a culture distinct from the organization's?", "answer": "Regardless of leadership and management style, one core factor for the wide acceptance of the project vision, mission and strategy is a conducive and supportive project culture. Typically, project culture spills over from the organizational culture, but PMs, especially in long-term projects, have a unique opportunity to create their own culture that can be quite distinct from the organization's (Kucharska & Kowalczyk, 2016). Viewing projects as temporary organizations with a culture that develops over the life of the project allows associated employees to understand what is valued, what has status, and what behaviors are expected."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "Describe the structural view of culture and its four culture types. What is the coursebook inconsistency? (mnemonic: Penguins Rarely Take Planes)", "answer": "The structural and interpretive views of culture were among the first attempts to frame culture theoretically (Hughes, Rana & Dwivedi, 2019). The structural view focuses on how roles are structured in a project: Power cultures (usually small projects): communication is informal and in compliance with the attitudes and norms of the central power source (usually the PM). Role cultures (usually larger and more formal projects): coordination provided by the leadership/elite, clear roles for team members reflected in their job descriptions. Task cultures (usually interdisciplinary projects): power lies at the intersection of roles. Person culture (usually professional organizations): the individual is the key element. INCONSISTENCY: exam version: the 4.1 text names the fourth type 'culture type'; correct version: 'person culture' (as in the 6.5 slides). In an essay write 'person culture (called culture type in the coursebook)'. The interpretive view is only named in 4.1 and explained in 6.5."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "How can culture be shaped directly and indirectly? Explain norms, rituals and stories. (mnemonic: No Rain Stops)", "answer": "Direct: setting up formal statements like the vision and mission, and communicating across the project team. Indirect ways typical of social groups that persist over time (Sharif, Yang, Kanwal & Shah, 2018): Norms: the way things are done in the organization; rules, procedures and standards. Rituals: activities that provide a sense of belonging and inclusion, e.g. celebrations, organizational retreats, performances and other activities that encourage teamwork. Stories: points of view as narratives of past events; their effect is often subliminal and greatly overlooked, but they can be powerful forces that shape and strengthen work culture while emphasizing its values (Clarke, Waring & Timmons, 2019). Stories anchor the organization's past to its present (the project), strengthen the organization by revealing the history and development of the culture, add back content that helps people reflect and learn as they envision themselves as the 'heroes' of a situation, and provide an alternative way of telling people what to do by cultivating expectations without actually providing instructions."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "Why do efforts to enforce a new culture often fail in projects, and what must PMs do?", "answer": "Reasons include underestimating the power of vision and not communicating about the new culture properly. Team members will not commit to a change effort unless they believe real benefits will result and that the change is achievable; for this they need sufficient information. PMs need to place great effort into removing any obstacles that block the project vision so the team does not slip back into old comfort zones."},
  {"tag": "4.1", "question": "LONG ANSWER (18 points): Explain the role of vision keeping in IT projects: why teams need it, vision vs mission vs strategy, leadership styles and their combination, and how project culture supports the vision.", "answer": "IT project teams are temporary (transient, part-time members), multidisciplinary, cross-cultural and often geographically dispersed with virtual limits (Niazi et al.), so they need strong ties and motivation: the will to perform, driving effort and persistence, based on members' values and aspirations (Patanakul et al.); three factors: achievement, affiliation, power. A project vision bonds the team in a shared future (Shenhar), clarifies purpose, eliminates confusion about direction and goals, and gives focus and belonging. Vision = all-encompassing, uplifting, belief-forming proposition with emotional weight; vision statement = concise declaration acting as a mantra, formalizing the big picture, in action-deliverable-criteria form (cRobot: design / robot / carry a warm cup of coffee; the inspirational 'smart assistant that cares' version adds AI and service-level ideas). Mission = vision in practical terms, concrete, goal-oriented, measurable, guiding daily activities and serving as benchmark; vision is the external mantra for all stakeholders, mission the internal one for the team. Strategy = short and long-term goals and how to achieve them, focused on present actions (Tonchia); communicated top-down from the PM. Leadership (PMBOK) = developing a vision and strategy and motivating people toward it. Literature is mostly transactional (routine, order, structure; exception Keegan & Hartog). Transformational: inspiring through a strong vision and a culture of trust and empowerment. Transactional: a leader-follower transaction, rewards-based, closer to management, expectations tied to rewards, dependency-building. New models are needed for temporary project teams. Blended styles (Harkiolakis): adapt to situations, flexibility (personal interaction plus authority), communicate vision at organizational level, high expectations plus core values; risks: wrong style applied by inexperienced leaders, perceived manipulation, instability from abrupt switching. Leaders communicate the vision; managers communicate objectives; roles overlap depending on life cycle phase. Figure 26: alternate empowerment and control; praise and recognition blend personal (transformational) and impersonal (transactional) interaction; use transactional as foundation, transformational for motivation and support. Culture: a conducive culture is core to accepting vision, mission and strategy; it spills over from the organization, but PMs of long-term projects can create a distinct one (Kucharska & Kowalczyk) showing what is valued, has status and is expected. Structural view (Hughes et al.): power (small, informal, PM-centred), role (large, formal, elite coordination, job descriptions), task (interdisciplinary, power at role intersections), person (professional, individual key; book: 'culture type'). Shaping: direct (vision/mission statements, communication) and indirect (norms, rituals, stories). Failure: underestimating vision and poor communication; members commit only if benefits are real and achievable and they have sufficient information; PMs remove obstacles so teams do not slip into old comfort zones."},
  
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "Define a stakeholder (PMBOK) and explain what 'affect' means, with the book example.", "answer": "Learn verbatim: 'any individual, group, or organization who may affect, be affected by, or perceive itself to be affected by a decision, activity, or outcome of a project' (Larson & Gray, 2015, p. 29). Modern organizations operate in an environment affected by a multitude of stakeholders. By 'affect' we consider conflicting interests that may result in positive or negative influences on the project and by the project. Example: a project that negatively impacts the environment could impact society and individuals in its vicinity; they in turn can protest and impact the direction of the project. (tutor-only) Likely exam question: define stakeholders and name all stakeholders you can think of for a short case description."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "Describe Figure 27 (Project Stakeholders from the Leader's Perspective) and what the outer layer represents.", "answer": "Figure-only content: nested layers with the PM at the centre, then the team, then the sponsor; then internal stakeholders (management, HR, manufacturing/production, accounting, finance); then the outer cloud of external stakeholders (government, public, regulations, competitors, clients, suppliers, partners, organizations). Examining the pressure each layer exerts on its internal layer gives a view of the entities affecting leaders' actions and goals at an instance in time. At the edges of the sphere of influence are those external to the organization (clients, suppliers, partners, competitors); they create conditions that can be opportunities or threats for the organization and, by extension, the PM; they provide the sources of the signals the environment sends to the organization and are the subjects of the impact the organization makes in the world. (tutor-only) PM, team and sponsor are the core; other departments are internal stakeholders; entities outside the company are external."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "What does managing stakeholders include, who is responsible, and why is engagement a key project objective? (mnemonic: I Analyze Engagement)", "answer": "Managing stakeholders is the prime responsibility of project managers and their proxies. It includes Identifying the various entities that affect the project, Analyzing their expectations and their power in influencing the project, and developing strategies for Engaging them in decision-making during the life cycle (Eskerod, Huemann & Savage, 2015). The last requires continuous communication to ensure the team understands needs and expectations; it also helps address issues as they occur. Fostering engagement in the various phases helps maintain their support and understanding, especially when conflicting interests must be managed. This is a key project objective because the product or service requires the approval of the core stakeholders and acceptance by the others."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "Why is stakeholder identification a key activity, and where does information on stakeholders come from?", "answer": "Identification allows the PM to identify the appropriate focus for each stakeholder and plan strategies to attend to their needs or alleviate the pressure they might exert on the project (van Offenbeek & Vos, 2016). Sources: the project charter (from the planning phase) provides information on internal and external entities, including the project sponsor or PMO, the customer, the team and any organizational departments involved; the project resources provide a list of suppliers, partners, consultants and any other entity that will interact with the project; additionally, the government (local and national) and the industry might exert influence through regulations (financial, environmental, etc.), product standards, etc."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "Explain the power-interest grid (Figure 28): the four quadrants, their actions and the book examples. (mnemonic: Many Smart Men Inform)", "answer": "A stakeholder analysis classifies stakeholders' interests and the amount of power they have in influencing project direction and outcome. High power, high interest (upper right): MANAGE CLOSELY; critical for project success, as they can easily ensure project failure if not managed closely (Eskerod et al., 2015); e.g. the project sponsor or the sign-off authority on deliverables, who should be continually informed of progress and alignment with objectives and goals. High power, low interest (upper left): KEEP SATISFIED; e.g. the customer, regulators, the CEO or CFO; they need to be satisfied but do not necessarily need continuous communication or attention; too much attention might have a negative effect; balancing their information needs and frequency of communication is best decided case by case in response to their initiatives. Low power, high interest (bottom right): KEEP INFORMED; e.g. suppliers, internal users, developers; very interested but limited power over direction; informed regularly, no special attention or consideration needed. Low power, low interest (bottom left): MONITOR; supporting personnel like system admins, technicians, helpdesk; least concerned with the project; cannot be ignored completely, but their priority is the lowest. Trap: the book places the customer under keep satisfied (high power, low interest). (tutor-only) Keep your power-interest analysis confidential; never share it with the stakeholders themselves."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "MCQ (IU slide): In the power-interest grid classification, stakeholders with low power and low interest should be...\nA) kept informed\nB) kept satisfied\nC) managed closely\nD) monitored", "answer": "D) monitored."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "Besides the power-interest grid, which other grid models exist, and what is their common limitation?", "answer": "Power/influence grid: influence represents the stakeholders' active involvement in the project. Influence/impact grid: impact represents their ability to effect changes in the planning or execution of the project. Grid-style models limit the classification to two dimensions; the salient model uses three (power, legitimacy, urgency)."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "What is the salient model (Figure 29)? Define legitimacy and urgency, and describe all seven stakeholder types with examples.", "answer": "The salient model (Mitchell et al., 1997) combines power with legitimacy and urgency. Legitimacy: the authority and genuineness of the involvement that stakeholders have on a project. Urgency: the degree to which stakeholder needs call for immediate attention. It is depicted as a Venn diagram with circles for each assessment parameter; the compartments represent seven types of stakeholders, some with multiple needs. Core/definite (power + urgency + legitimacy, centre): require special attention and close management. Dangerous (power + urgency): have the power to interfere while lacking the legitimacy to do so, e.g. radical elements like hackers in IT projects. Dependent (legitimacy + urgency): low power, so no close management, but their presence should not be ignored as they could easily affiliate with power centers and influence the project, e.g. communities near a project with significant environmental impact. Dominant (legitimacy + power): require close monitoring but not as close attention as core, e.g. compliance bodies that audit the project outcome for compliance with standards. Stakeholders with only one attribute are latent; besides dormant ones, they demand lower attention because their impact is minimal. Dormant (power only): e.g. the CEO; might have no direct interest but must be watched, as they have the power to dramatically affect the project. Discretionary (legitimacy only): e.g. NGOs; legitimate interest but no power. Demanding (urgency only): high urgency but low legitimacy or power, e.g. a social group affected by the project outcome. (tutor-only) Know there are 7 types, a brief definition of each, and how to deal with them. Tutor error dropped: she named 'agency' instead of urgency."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "MCQ: In the salient model, a stakeholder with power and urgency but no legitimacy is called...\nA) dominant\nB) dangerous\nC) dependent\nD) dormant", "answer": "B) dangerous (e.g. hackers). Dominant = power + legitimacy; dependent = legitimacy + urgency; dormant = power only."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "What are the advantages and disadvantages of the salient model, and what should dictate model choice?", "answer": "Advantage: it allows the project team to gain better insight into the stakeholders and dedicate the appropriate resources, time and effort to their needs. Disadvantages compared to other models: it requires more time and effort to build and maintain; it does not provide any insight into the weights of each attribute, as a subjective evaluation is not immune to bias. The needs of the project in terms of stakeholder participation and engagement should dictate the choice of model. Regardless of the model, stakeholder needs are dynamic and can change during the project life cycle."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "Explain the RACI matrix: purpose, the four roles with examples, and how it relates to other planning tools. Describe Figure 30.", "answer": "Stakeholder classification is often supplemented with a role-based approach that considers stakeholder responsibilities (Desmond, 2017). RACI (model/matrix/chart) defines and documents project roles and responsibilities; four core roles: Responsible: stakeholders who do the work, e.g. the PM, team members and any partners or groups assigned a task. Accountable: stakeholders who have ownership of the work; they approve a completed task or achieved objective; best assigned to only one person, typically the PM. Consulted: stakeholders who need to give input when requested; typically kept informed and active; e.g. compliance consultants. Informed: need to know how the project progresses but are not consulted and do not contribute directly; e.g. the CEO. RACI matrices are developed with work distribution in mind, so they complement the WBS and scheduling (e.g. Gantt charts). Figure 30 (figure-only): columns Sponsor, PM, Analyst, Developer, Tester, Admin, User, Supplier; rows Tasks 1-4; the PM is A on every task (one accountable person); Developer is R on tasks 1-3, Analyst R on task 1, Tester R,C on task 3; cells can hold combined roles like 'C, I'. Confirmed key: RACI includes 'Responsible'."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "MCQ (IU slide): The RACI model considers four core roles and responsibilities a stakeholder can play, including...\nA) required, accessible, critical, and informed\nB) responsible, accessible, critical, and informed\nC) responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed\nD) required, accountable, consulted, and informed", "answer": "C) responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "What comes after stakeholder classification? What is stakeholder management really about, and what does a stakeholder register contain? (mnemonic: Hard Data Is Fixed, Soft Data Shifts)", "answer": "After classifying stakeholders, the next step is developing a plan to address their needs, engage them in the project and manage their expectations to ultimately achieve the project objectives (Larson & Gray, 2015). Stakeholder management is about building and strengthening relationships, not just improving communications and managing a team. An effective plan is critical and must be dynamic, given that participation and engagement vary during the project. A stakeholder register is typically developed to include their profiles and the tools used for profiling them: hard data like contact information, department, role in the project, knowledge level; soft data like influence, expectations, key requirements, and when the stakeholder's involvement is most critical. It also includes the classification of the tools used and an action plan with appropriate ways to engage stakeholders at each phase of the life cycle."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "Explain the stakeholder engagement assessment matrix (Table 4): the five engagement levels, the C/D notation and the book's example. What are the inconsistencies? (mnemonic: Ugly Rats Never Stop Leaping)", "answer": "A stakeholder engagement matrix identifies stakeholders' needs in terms of their level of engagement. Unaware: unaware of the project and its potential impact. Resistant: aware of the project and its potential impact but resistant to change. Neutral: aware of the project but neither supportive nor resistant. Supportive: aware of the project and its potential impact and supportive of change. Leading: actively engaged in the project's success. Each stakeholder's engagement at each stage is marked based on available information: 'C' = currently engaged, 'D' = desired engagement. Book example: S3 is at the desired engagement level; S1 requires further communication and additional actions to reach it; S2 and S4 are engaged in some aspects and tasks but require more attention to get engaged in others. Inconsistencies: (1) the tutor omits neutral; the book has five levels, so always write all five. (2) In the extracted Table 4 the letters do not clearly match the text reading (S4 appears as 'CD' in one cell); use the book's text reading in answers."},
  {"tag": "4.2", "question": "LONG ANSWER (18 points): Explain stakeholder management in IT projects: definition, identification, classification models (power-interest grid, other grids, salient model), RACI, the stakeholder register and the engagement assessment matrix.", "answer": "Stakeholder (PMBOK): any individual, group or organization who may affect, be affected by, or perceive itself to be affected by a decision, activity or outcome of a project; 'affect' covers conflicting interests with positive or negative influence (e.g. an environmentally harmful project triggering protests). Figure 27: layers around the leader (PM, team, sponsor, internal departments such as management, HR, production, accounting, finance; external entities such as government, public, regulations, competitors, clients, suppliers, partners), each layer pressuring the inner one; external entities create opportunities and threats and are sources of environmental signals. Managing stakeholders is the PM's prime responsibility (Eskerod et al.): identify entities, analyze expectations and power, develop strategies to engage them in decision-making via continuous communication; engagement keeps support, especially with conflicting interests, and is a key objective because outputs need core stakeholders' approval and others' acceptance. Identification sets the focus for each stakeholder (van Offenbeek & Vos), using the project charter (sponsor/PMO, customer, team, departments), project resources (suppliers, partners, consultants) and government/industry (regulations, standards). Power-interest grid: high/high manage closely (sponsor, sign-off authority; can ensure failure); high power/low interest keep satisfied (customer, regulators, CEO/CFO; too much attention harms; case by case); low power/high interest keep informed (suppliers, internal users, developers); low/low monitor (sysadmins, technicians, helpdesk; lowest priority). Other grids: power/influence (active involvement) and influence/impact (ability to change planning or execution); grids are limited to two dimensions. Salient model (Mitchell et al.): power plus legitimacy (authority and genuineness of involvement) and urgency (need for immediate attention), drawn as a Venn diagram with 7 types: core/definite (all three; special attention), dangerous (power + urgency; hackers), dependent (legitimacy + urgency; nearby communities; can affiliate with power centers), dominant (power + legitimacy; compliance bodies; close monitoring), and latent single-attribute types: dormant (power; CEO; watch), discretionary (legitimacy; NGOs), demanding (urgency; affected social group). It gives better insight for allocating resources, but takes more time to build and maintain, gives no attribute weights, and is subjective and biased; project needs dictate model choice; needs are dynamic. RACI (Desmond): responsible (does the work), accountable (owns and approves; one person, typically the PM), consulted (gives input; compliance consultants), informed (kept updated; CEO); complements WBS and Gantt. Planning: a plan to address needs, engage and manage expectations (Larson & Gray); stakeholder management is about building and strengthening relationships, not just communication; the plan is dynamic. Stakeholder register: profiles, hard data (contacts, department, role, knowledge level) and soft data (influence, expectations, key requirements, critical involvement timing), tools, and an action plan per phase. Engagement assessment matrix: unaware, resistant, neutral, supportive, leading, with C (current) and D (desired) marked to find gaps (S3 at desired; S1 needs more communication and actions; S2/S4 partially engaged)."},

  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "Define conflict. How does it differ from a debate and a discussion, and can conflict be positive?", "answer": "Learn verbatim: conflict is an intense disagreement or argument, typically a protracted one. It differs from a debate in that it has no formal structure, and is far from a discussion, which focuses on an exchange of ideas in order to reach a decision. Conflicts can be healthy and productive, e.g. when we brainstorm to come up with a solution or develop operational procedures to improve performance, but the great majority are destructive in nature and can damage a project when they occur."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "What are the sources of conflict in projects, and what influences the extent and level of conflict? (mnemonics: Tired Cats Sleep In Cardboard / I Think It Matters)", "answer": "Sources: Task-related (e.g. internal competition for limited resources); related to Checks and balances; Scheduling priorities; Interpersonal issues like different working styles and cultural perspectives; agreement/Contractual responsibilities and accountability. Extent and level are influenced by: the relative importance and Intensity of the issue that creates the conflict; Time pressure/constraints; the Importance of the issues to the involved actors; those actors' Motivation to resolve the conflict on a long or short-term basis."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "Name and explain the five conflict resolution techniques (Larson & Gray, 2015), including when each is ideal and its drawbacks. (mnemonic: We Fight, Compromise, Collaborate, Smooth)", "answer": "Withdraw/avoid: the no-engagement strategy; we retreat/postpone engagement and allow others to resolve their issues; might seem defeatist but is ideal when the issue crosses the boundaries of our expertise or power. Force/direct: the opposite; we impose our will on the other side (probably the weaker in terms of power); a win-lose solution (we win everything, the other side nothing); sounds brutal but is ideal for emergency situations where a leader needs to exercise power for an immediate response. Compromise/reconcile: looking for the middle ground where some degree of satisfaction is sought for all parties; resolves the conflict temporarily or partially, but in the long run residual issues could surface as another conflict. Collaborate/problem-solve: considering different perspectives and viewpoints to reach consensus and resolve an issue; ensures the commitment of the involved parties but takes longer to get everyone on board and analyze all alternatives. Smooth/accommodate: the stoic approach where we generally concede our position for the benefit of the whole; promotes harmony and good relationships by focusing on similarities rather than differences; improves team cohesion as long as the underlying issue is not persistent and does not surface regularly."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "MCQ: Which conflict resolution technique is ideal for emergency situations requiring an immediate response?\nA) Withdraw/avoid\nB) Force/direct\nC) Compromise/reconcile\nD) Smooth/accommodate", "answer": "B) Force/direct: a win-lose solution, ideal when a leader must exercise power for an immediate response. Withdraw/avoid is ideal when the issue crosses the boundaries of our expertise or power."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "MCQ: Which technique ensures the commitment of the involved parties but takes longer?\nA) Compromise/reconcile\nB) Collaborate/problem-solve\nC) Smooth/accommodate\nD) Force/direct", "answer": "B) Collaborate/problem-solve. Compromise only resolves conflict temporarily or partially with possible residual issues; smoothing promotes harmony as long as the issue is not persistent."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "Why does the coursebook treat negotiation as a core skill of project managers?", "answer": "The five techniques show a general breakdown of strategies, but there is no one-size-fits-all solution; even within individual strategies, success relies on someone's ability to negotiate. Every human interaction can be seen as a form of negotiation where we trade something of value (money, effort, attention, a product or a service) for something we need (other products or services, someone else's effort, money or attention). Even love is a form of exchange (excitement, fulfilment, inspiration, safety, security, care). This makes negotiation a universal practice and, from the leader/PM's perspective, a core skill for fulfilling their role."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "Which disciplines do negotiation processes blend, what do they involve, and what is the role of leaders in negotiation? (mnemonic: Good People Play Cards, Like Silly Ants)", "answer": "Negotiation processes are traditionally a blend of game theory, psychology, political science, communications, law, sociology and anthropology. They involve a trade-off between gains and losses due to the negotiators' positions; some are planned, others catch those involved by surprise. Questions to consider include: What are my priorities? How far can I go? Why is the other side following a certain strategy? Would I do the same? Are they honest and truthful? How do they understand commitment? Should I start the bidding first? Should I be aggressive? Should we spend more time getting to know each other? Should I walk away? Should I make more concessions? The answers shape the strategy and lead to the success or failure of the negotiation. Strategy and behavior become expressions of each other; establishing one can determine the other. Leaders function as optimizers, identifying the best solutions considering their own needs, the other side's needs and what is available to both; based on their perception of the environment they plan their strategy and adapt their tactics minute by minute according to the stakes (Harkiolakis & Halkias, 2016). The target is to maximize short-term and long-term gains, keeping in mind that the outcome impacts the individuals as professionals and the organizations they represent."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "Explain leaders as knowledge systems and Figure 31 (Influence Layers During Message Exchange). (mnemonic: Good Families Cook Well)", "answer": "Leaders who negotiate can be viewed as knowledge systems influenced and shaped by different environmental factors as they mature as individuals and professionals. Starting with DNA, we are raised in family environments that operate in societies, which are microcosms within nations, regions and the world. Each environmental layer encloses and exerts influence primarily on those it encloses and, to a lesser degree, on its outside layers (Harkiolakis, 2016): as we are influenced by the world, we also influence the world, at least in our own vicinity; for a person this is expressed as education and experience while interacting with others. Figure 31 (figure-only): two people in different parts of the world, each surrounded by rings of Genes, Family and Culture within the World; each sends a message (arrow) that must pass through these layers. Messages we send and receive go through layers of influence that can include our heritage (genetic, social or cultural) and our environment; the same is true for the other party. This filtering means negotiators must code their message to clearly reach the other party; decoding on the receiver side is also vital unless the other party is aware and trained enough to code their message so it is not polluted by the influence layers."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "Describe the negotiation framework and its four phases (Figure 32). What does the book say about skipping phases and rationality? (mnemonic: I Probably Should Call)", "answer": "A framework ensures all parameters of a negotiation issue and the parties' positions are understood and projected clearly; negotiation is a sequence of steps we consciously or unconsciously take to reach our goal (Harkiolakis et al., 2016); making it visible helps us become aware of what is going on and make the most of it. Intelligence phase: find as much information as possible on the issue and parties. Perception phase: make sense of what we found. Strategy: build the negotiation strategy based on that. Communication: execute the strategy by communicating our intentions and proposals. Transitions vary in speed; sometimes it appears we jump phases, but we actually go through them: even with all information in advance, we reassure ourselves the facts are complete (intelligence) and that we interpret them correctly (perception) before formulating strategy; we do not communicate without ensuring alignment with our strategy. Following such a process does not mean individual elements are rational; optimization of skills is a different issue related to our abilities, experience and the situation. Nothing is set in stone: understanding, flexibility and good judgment help negotiators choose their approach and strategy. Figure 32 (figure-only): a downward spiral over time through intelligence, perception, strategy and communication, loops becoming shorter, ending in Deal/No Deal."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "How does the length of each negotiation phase change over time?", "answer": "We traverse the spiral of intelligence, perception, strategy and communication until we reach an agreement or abandon the negotiation; the length of each phase is not constant. Intelligence: more time at the beginning when we are not familiar with the issue, less at the end when we understand the issues and variables from previous exchanges. Perception: expected to decrease over time as interaction and information exchange develop understanding. Communication, and to a lesser degree strategy, is expected to dominate as time goes on, with rapid exchanges of information to clarify issues and reach an agreement. Towards the end the loops become shorter until the final decision to commit to an agreement or walk away without a deal."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "MCQ: Which negotiation phase is expected to decrease over time as interaction with the counterpart develops?\nA) Communication\nB) Strategy\nC) Perception\nD) None, all phases remain constant", "answer": "C) Perception. Communication (and to a lesser degree strategy) dominates as time goes on; intelligence is longest at the beginning."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "Describe Figure 33 (Negotiation Dominated by the Communications Phase) and when it applies.", "answer": "Figure-only: the same spiral as Figure 32, but communication takes up most of every loop from the start, with very short intelligence, perception and strategy segments, ending in Deal/No Deal. It applies to negotiations we encounter frequently or cases we are familiar with: we spend very little time on intelligence, perception and strategy and quickly move to the communication phase, where most of the time is spent. This is natural and expected, but we should occasionally reflect on the suitability of our applied strategies even in routine negotiations, since exceptions might appear that require additional consideration."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "How does information technology affect negotiations? Define e-negotiations and state their advantages. (mnemonic: Information Supports Participation)", "answer": "With many negotiations online, IT is an additional variable. Technology can affect communications and increase negotiators' capabilities by making it easy to acquire information, providing support with negotiation decision support systems, and expanding stakeholder participation, especially with the growth of social media. E-negotiations: a subset of negotiations that rely exclusively on technology. Sidebar: conducted using only electronic means, allowing more efficiency but creating difficulty with body language and facial cues. Major advantage: the ability to record every detail of the communication process (intentions, outcomes, information exchanged), giving better understanding of the negotiation process and behavior and enabling analysis and evaluation of strategies that would be impossible face-to-face. The time lag from asynchronous communication has been shown to make negotiators pay more attention to the substantive content of messages, reduce the emotional stress of conflicting positions, and make it easier to overcome socioeconomic differences."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "What challenges do e-negotiations pose, and what should be considered before negotiating online? (mnemonic: Faces Cold, Direction Time)", "answer": "The challenges can frequently outweigh the benefits: the lack of face and body cues is very difficult for many to overcome (Harkiolakis et al., 2016); the online medium is considered cold and biased against non-native English speakers without fluency; typing left to right while someone reads right to left or top to bottom can cause profound differences in how things are expressed and interpreted; perceptions of time cause misinterpretations (e.g. languages with no future tense or no verb 'to be', even between variations of the same language). Negotiators still need knowledge, judgment and expertise; adaptation to the new realities is not a matter of choice but a survival requirement. Before negotiating online, consider whether tasks or people will be affected: tasks are impersonal and benefit from the structure and permanence of the online medium, while decisions about people need the expressive power of humans with their verbal and nonverbal cues."},
  {"tag": "4.3", "question": "LONG ANSWER (18 points): Explain conflict management in IT projects: definition, sources, influencing factors, resolution techniques, and the role of negotiation, including the negotiation framework and e-negotiations.", "answer": "Conflict = an intense, typically protracted disagreement; unlike a debate it has no formal structure, and unlike a discussion it is not an exchange of ideas to reach a decision; it can be productive (brainstorming, improving procedures) but mostly destroys and damages projects. Sources: task-related (competition for limited resources), checks and balances, scheduling priorities, interpersonal issues (working styles, cultural perspectives), contractual responsibilities and accountability. Extent depends on the issue's importance and intensity, time pressure, importance to actors, and their motivation to resolve it short or long term. Techniques (Larson & Gray): withdraw/avoid (retreat; ideal beyond our expertise or power), force/direct (impose will; win-lose; ideal in emergencies), compromise/reconcile (middle ground; partial or temporary, residual issues), collaborate/problem-solve (consensus from perspectives; commitment but slow), smooth/accommodate (concede for the whole; harmony and cohesion if the issue is not persistent). No one-size-fits-all: success relies on negotiation ability; every interaction is an exchange of value (even love), so negotiation is a core PM skill. Negotiation blends game theory, psychology, political science, communications, law, sociology and anthropology; trade-off of gains and losses; answers to questions about priorities, limits and honesty shape strategy; strategy and behavior determine each other. Leaders optimize across own needs, other side's needs and availability, adapting tactics minute by minute (Harkiolakis & Halkias) to maximize short- and long-term gains. Leaders as knowledge systems: DNA, family, society, nations, regions, world, each layer influencing mainly the inner layers; messages pass through genes, family and culture on both sides (Figure 31), so negotiators must code and decode carefully. Framework (Harkiolakis et al.): intelligence (gather information), perception (make sense), strategy (build), communication (execute); phases are never truly skipped; process does not guarantee rationality; flexibility and judgment matter. Over time (Figure 32) intelligence shrinks after the start, perception decreases, communication and to a lesser degree strategy dominate, loops shorten until deal or no deal; in routine negotiations communication dominates throughout (Figure 33), but strategies should still be reflected on. E-negotiations rely exclusively on technology; IT eases information acquisition, enables decision support systems and widens participation; advantages: full records enabling analysis, and asynchronous time lag that focuses on content, reduces emotional stress and eases socioeconomic differences; challenges: missing face and body cues, cold medium biased against non-native English speakers, writing direction, differing time perceptions. Adaptation is a survival requirement; online suits impersonal task decisions, while decisions about people need verbal and nonverbal cues."}


]
